from gen import D
TOUCH={
"oxycodone":"Immediate-release oxycodone tablets from US generic makers are mostly small, round, flat tablets with a smooth surface, often with a score line across one side and a debossed (pressed-in) imprint you can sometimes feel. OxyContin extended-release tablets are small, round, bi-convex (domed on both sides), and film-coated, so they feel slick and hard, with no score line; the DailyMed label lists them as debossed 'OP' on one side and the strength on the other. Percocet and its generics are larger, round or oblong tablets, often scored. Oxycodone oral solution is a liquid.",
"hydrocodone":"Hydrocodone-acetaminophen tablets (Norco and generics) are fairly large because of the acetaminophen. Per the DailyMed label for one generic maker, the lowest strength is a capsule-shaped tablet with a score line (bisected) on one side, while some other strengths are unscored ovals; all have a debossed code you may feel. Hysingla ER is an oblong, film-coated tablet; Zohydro ER is a capsule.",
"buprenorphine":"Suboxone film (DailyMed: an orange rectangular film with a printed logo) is a thin, flexible rectangle, about the size of a postage stamp, that sticks to the mouth lining as it dissolves; each film comes in its own child-resistant foil pouch you tear open. Generic buprenorphine and buprenorphine-naloxone tablets are small, flat, and either round or oval, dissolving under the tongue with a gritty, bitter feel. Zubsolv tablets are small with a menthol taste. Butrans is a square-ish skin patch.",
"fentanyl":"Fentanyl patches are thin, flexible, rectangular or rounded-rectangle adhesive patches sealed in individual pouches; larger patches deliver more drug. Actiq is a solid lozenge on a plastic handle, like a lollipop. Fentora is a flat, round buccal tablet.",
"methadone":"Methadone tablets are usually small, round or rectangular, and scored; dispersible tablets (Diskets) are larger, flat, and cross-scored so they can be broken. Clinic methadone is usually a liquid, often colored and flavored, given in a small cup.",
"morphine":"MS Contin tablets are round and film-coated (DailyMed), feeling smooth and hard, with a debossed 'PF' symbol on one side. Immediate-release morphine tablets are often scored. Kadian is a capsule containing small pellets. Morphine oral solution is a liquid.",
"hydromorphone":"Per the DailyMed Dilaudid label, the lower-strength tablets are small, round, and flat-faced with beveled edges and a debossed 'P', while the highest-strength tablet is triangular with a score line between two debossed 'P' letters. Generics vary. Extended-release tablets are film-coated.",
"tramadol":"Per DailyMed labels, many generic immediate-release tramadol tablets are white, round, and film-coated, so they feel smooth; some strengths have a score line, others are plain on one side. Shapes differ between manufacturers (some are oblong or capsule-shaped). Extended-release tablets are unscored.",
"codeine":"Acetaminophen-codeine tablets (Tylenol #3 and generics) are usually round, white, fairly large tablets with a debossed imprint. Codeine cough syrups are liquids.",
}
WARN="""**Touch cannot confirm what a pill is.** Different manufacturers make the same medicine in different shapes and sizes, and counterfeit pills, such as fake 'M30' oxycodone 'blues' or fake Xanax 'bars', are pressed to imitate real ones and often contain fentanyl or designer benzodiazepines. Safer ways to check: ask a pharmacist (they can identify a pill from its imprint), use a pill-identifier app or a screen-reader-friendly service such as Be My Eyes or Seeing AI to read the imprint aloud and look it up on DailyMed or Drugs.com, keep medicines in labeled pharmacy bottles, and use fentanyl test strips on anything not from a pharmacy."""
def touch(slug):
    t=D[slug].get("touch") or TOUCH.get(slug) or "Forms vary by country and manufacturer; see the brand list above. Ask a pharmacist to describe the product you have."
    return f"## {D[slug].get('touchh','What the pills feel like')}\n\n{t}\n\n{WARN}\n\n"
